Joules per frame for BiSeNetV2 (512x512 segmentation) -- a second model family, chosen
because it is the case where this repo's own docs record the NPU's latency lead over the
iGPU as narrowest (1.09x DML). The energy finding survives that test decisively: **the NPU
runs 3.5-5.9x more efficiently than the iGPU and an order of magnitude more efficiently
than the CPU, even though the raw-speed race between NPU and iGPU is close enough to flip
direction between sittings.**
Desktop 2 (Ryzen 7 8700G / Phoenix XDNA1 / Radeon 780M), DESKTOP-CBL5NUA, 2026-09-10,
08:53-09:07. Six power phases in one sitting, 60 s sampled each, 15 s settle between
phases; throughput measured in a FULLY SEPARATE pass afterward (see METHODOLOGY -- this is
the fix for the contamination `power_rapl_resnet50_joules_per_frame.log` found and
retracted). `xrt-smi` clean before and after.

WHY THIS MODEL
==============
`power_rapl_resnet50_joules_per_frame.log` established the shape of the finding on a
classification CNN where the NPU already wins comfortably on latency (2.80x over CPU,
1.34x over iGPU). That leaves open whether the energy edge is a byproduct of also being
faster, or whether it holds independently. BiSeNetV2 is the sharpest available test:
`docs/BENCHMARKS.md`'s own Category B result puts the NPU only 1.09x ahead of DML on
latency, the closest race in this repo between those two providers on any model.

THE IDLE BASELINE HERE IS NOISY, AND THAT IS REPORTED RATHER THAN HIDDEN
=========================================================================
Unlike the ResNet50 sitting, where idle phases A and F agreed to 0.8% on the median, here
they disagree by **41%** (A 18.846 W, F 26.610 W median package power). This is not a
fluke of one sample: the RANGE within each idle phase is itself unusually wide (A: 15.35-
39.90 W, a 24.55 W spread; F: 16.53-64.78 W, a 48.25 W spread), while every ACTIVE workload
phase is comparatively tight (7.50-22.37 W spread). That pattern -- noise concentrated in
the idle phases, not the busy ones -- is consistent with background OS activity (Defender,
Search indexing, telemetry, Windows Update checks) being a large relative perturbation
against a near-zero true-idle floor and a small one against an already-busy package; it is
not chased further than that observation. The peer process from the two prior studies
(PID 22396) had exited before this sitting, which may itself be relevant -- with no
sustained ~1-core background load holding the package off its deepest idle states, the
package may swing more between deep-idle and OS-housekeeping bursts.

**Consequence: no single idle baseline is asserted.** Every energy figure below is reported
as a range across the two plausible baselines (the lower and higher of the A/F medians)
plus their mean, rather than as one point estimate the way the ResNet50 log could.

WHAT WAS RUN
============
Same instruments as the ResNet50 study: `tools/power_probe.py` (RAPL via PDH) and
`tools/session_hold.py --ep {npu,cpu,dml}`. Models: `bisenetv2_fp32.onnx` (B, C) and
`bisenetv2_fp32_xint8.onnx` (D, E), cache key `bisenetv2cachekey`, single stream, batch 1,
512x512x3 in / 512x512x19 logits out.

  A idle -> B CPU fp32 -> C iGPU/DML fp32 -> D NPU xint8 -> E CPU xint8 -> F idle again
  (then, separately, in its own pass with 8 s settle between arms:)
  throughput: B -> C -> E -> D

RESULT
======
Medians, all six phases:

  phase                 package    cores sum   residual    fps (clean pass)
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A idle                 18.846 W     3.514 W    15.332 W     --
  B CPU fp32             77.937 W    53.024 W    24.913 W    20.7
  C iGPU (DML) fp32      76.283 W     7.942 W    68.341 W    79.3
  D NPU xint8            34.557 W     7.021 W    27.536 W    75.1
  E CPU xint8 (control)  63.325 W    42.090 W    21.235 W    11.5
  F idle again           26.610 W     9.694 W    16.916 W     --

Because the idle range spans 18.846-26.610 W, energy figures are given across that range
(low / mean / high) rather than at one point:

  arm                   J/frame @idle_lo   @idle_mean   @idle_hi
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
  CPU fp32                    2.8546          2.6671       2.4796
  iGPU (DML) fp32             0.7243          0.6754       0.6264
  **NPU xint8**               **0.2092**      **0.1575**   **0.1058**
  CPU xint8 (control)         3.8677          3.5302       3.1926

  NPU vs CPU fp32  : 13.65x (idle_lo) -- 16.93x (mean) -- 23.43x (idle_hi)
  NPU vs iGPU      :  3.46x (idle_lo) --  4.29x (mean) --  5.92x (idle_hi)
  NPU vs CPU xint8 : 18.49x (idle_lo) -- 22.41x (mean) -- 30.17x (idle_hi)

**Across the ENTIRE plausible baseline range, the ranking never changes and the NPU never
drops below 3.46x the iGPU or 13.65x the CPU.** The exact multiple is uncertain by roughly
1.7x end to end (a direct consequence of the idle noise above); the order of magnitude and
the ranking (NPU >> iGPU > CPU) are not.

THE SHARPEST FINDING: LATENCY IS A COIN FLIP, ENERGY IS NOT
=============================================================
This session's clean throughput pass reads NPU 75.1 fps against iGPU 79.3 fps -- **the
iGPU is 1.056x FASTER than the NPU here**, the opposite direction from
`docs/BENCHMARKS.md`'s recorded 1.086x NPU-over-DML for the same model family. Both
figures are close enough to 1.0 that this is a coin flip across sittings, consistent with
this repo's own documented finding that NPU/DML latency drifts session to session (the
12.7 ms vs 6.9 ms example already in `CLAUDE.md`) -- **not** claimed here as a regression,
just as two independent measurements of a near-tie.

The energy result does not share that ambiguity at any point in the sensitivity range: the
NPU is **3.46x-5.92x more efficient than the iGPU regardless of which sitting's latency
number you believe.** This is the cleanest available evidence in this repo that the energy
advantage is not merely riding on a speed advantage -- on the one model where speed alone
would call the NPU a wash or a narrow loss against the iGPU, energy calls it decisively.

THE DTYPE CONTROL REPRODUCES
=============================
As with ResNet50, the CPU-on-the-same-INT8-artifact control moves the WRONG way for the
"int8 is just cheaper" hypothesis: CPU xint8 is **slower** than CPU fp32 (11.5 vs 20.7 fps)
and **worse on energy at every baseline** (3.53 vs 2.67 J/frame at the mean). ONNX
Runtime's QDQ int8 path costs this CPU throughput AND energy on a second, structurally
different model (segmentation, not classification), which is a second independent
instance of the same pessimization `power_rapl_resnet50_joules_per_frame.log` found.

ATTRIBUTION IS NOT AS CLEAN HERE AS IN THE RESNET50 SITTING, AND THAT IS STATED PLAINLY
=========================================================================================
The ResNet50 log could say cleanly "iGPU cores drop below idle, NPU cores barely move"
because that sitting's idle floor was tight and well below every workload's core reading.
Here the idle core median itself ranges from 3.51 W (A) to 9.69 W (F) -- overlapping the
NPU phase's own 7.02 W core median and close to the iGPU phase's 7.94 W. **No confident
core-vs-residual attribution is drawn from this sitting alone.** The package-level ranking
is unaffected (it does not depend on the core split), but readers should look to the
ResNet50 log for the provider-attribution mechanism, not this one.

METHODOLOGY -- the ResNet50 contamination did not recur
==========================================================
This run separates throughput measurement from power sampling entirely: all six power
phases run first (each with a 15 s settle after), THEN a fully independent throughput pass
runs each arm with an 8 s settle between them. No throughput figure here shows the
halving signature the ResNet50 study found and retracted (compare: this sitting's NPU fps
is 75.1 with `power_probe.py`'s own single post-compile sample reading 69.3 -- close, not
half -- so the fix holds). All four throughput readings are internally stable (12 one-
second intervals per arm; see raw output).

An unrelated workflow error occurred and is noted for completeness, not because it
affected any data: after this study completed, a follow-up shell invocation accidentally
re-ran the whole script with a stray `--help` argument (which the script does not parse,
so it just ran again) before being caught and killed a few dozen seconds in, inside phase
B of the restarted run. It was caught before phase A of the restart finished, two orphaned
`python.exe` processes were terminated, and `xrt-smi` confirmed no hardware context was
left open. It overwrote the standalone `.jsonl` phase file with a partial restart, which is
irrelevant because the complete original six-phase data was already captured in this log's
own stdout (below) before the restart began.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH
============================
- **The idle baseline uncertainty is the headline caveat.** A 41% idle-to-idle disagreement
  is a real limitation, not resolved here beyond bounding it with a sensitivity range.
  Repeating this sitting under `--pmode` fixed to a specific power plan, or sampling a
  longer idle window, are the obvious next steps if a tighter number is wanted.
- **One model, batch 1, single stream** -- same scope limits as the ResNet50 log.
- **Not sustained thermal steady state** -- 60 s windows.
- **Throughput and power still come from separate windows** minutes apart.
- **The NPU residual is an upper bound**, and the core-attribution argument that
  strengthened the ResNet50 reading does not transfer cleanly here (see above).
- **No accuracy axis** -- BiSeNetV2 XINT8's own known fixed-point fidelity issue on
  physical hardware (`docs/BENCHMARKS.md`, Category B) is a separate, already-documented
  finding and is not re-examined by this energy measurement.

