CORRECTION (2026-09-10, same session, before this log was ever cited externally)
================================================================================
Two things are corrected here. Neither changes the qualitative finding (NPU beats the
iGPU on energy even where its latency lead is a coin flip) but the quantitative range
above, and one methodology claim, are both wrong and are retracted.

1. THE 41% IDLE DISAGREEMENT WAS PROBABLY NOT AMBIENT NOISE -- IT WAS AN ORPHANED
   WORKLOAD FROM THE PRECEDING PHASE. `tools/power_probe.py` ended its `--command`
   workload with `proc.terminate()`. On Windows, `subprocess.Popen(cmd, shell=True)`
   makes the tracked PID a `cmd.exe` wrapper, and `TerminateProcess` on that PID does not
   touch its child -- confirmed directly on 2026-09-10 by sampling for 5 s against a 60 s
   held workload and finding both the `cmd.exe` and the `python.exe` underneath it still
   running, unkilled, after the tool's own 15 s `wait()` had already elapsed. That
   workload's 95 s hold outlives the tool's ~63 s active window by ~32 s -- long enough to
   bleed through this study's 15 s SETTLE gap and into the next phase's own sampling
   window. Phase F (idle again) is the most likely victim: its package **max, 64.8 W,
   matches phase E's (CPU on xint8) own package median of 63.3 W** almost exactly, and E
   immediately precedes F with no throughput pass between them to absorb the tail (unlike
   the ResNet50 study, see point 3). This is stated as "very likely," not certain -- it is
   not provable after the fact which samples in F's 59-sample window were contaminated,
   only that the magnitude and timing are consistent with E's orphan and inconsistent with
   ordinary background noise at this host's otherwise-idle floor.
   **Fixed** in `tools/power_probe.py`: the workload is now torn down with
   `taskkill /F /T /PID <pid>` (kills the whole process tree), verified by repeating the
   orphan test above and confirming no survivors. See `docs/DECISIONS.md`'s new pitfall
   entry for the general lesson.

2. THE RE-MEASUREMENT DID NOT SIMPLY CONVERGE, AND THAT IS INSTRUCTIVE ON ITS OWN.
   Re-running this study with the fix (`power_rapl_bisenetv2_joules_per_frame_v2.log`)
   did not tighten phase A and F onto one number -- they disagreed even more sharply, in
   the OPPOSITE direction (A high, F low), for an unrelated reason diagnosed in that log
   (a transient two-core load contaminated v2's phase A specifically; v2's phase F is used
   as the sole baseline, with a physical-impossibility argument for excluding A, not a
   range). So: the orphan-teardown bug was real and is fixed, but it was NOT the sole or
   even the primary source of idle-baseline instability on this host -- general background
   load turned out to be large enough on its own to need active diagnosis on every
   sitting, not just a range-averaging response. The **3.46-5.92x (iGPU) and
   13.65-23.43x (CPU) sensitivity range stated above is retracted**: it bracketed one
   contaminated and one clean-ish idle reading as if both were equally valid endpoints of
   real ambient variance, which the mechanism above shows was not the right model. The
   corrected point estimates, from the clean re-measurement's F baseline:
   **NPU ~5.0x more energy-efficient than the iGPU, ~18.6x more than the CPU** (marginal
   power over idle, medians, throughput from a fully separate clean pass). The dtype
   control and the coin-flip-latency finding both reproduce in the new log. See
   `power_rapl_bisenetv2_joules_per_frame_v2.log` for the full write-up and is the log to
   cite going forward; this one is superseded but kept for the orphan-mechanism evidence
   above.

3. A small factual error in this log's own METHODOLOGY section, unrelated to the above:
   it says the accidental `--help` re-run "was caught before phase A of the restart
   finished ... inside phase B of the restarted run" -- self-contradictory. The restart's
   partial `.jsonl` had `A_idle` and `B_cpu_fp32` written, so it was caught inside phase
   B, not phase A. No data was affected either way (the original six-phase run had
   already completed and been captured in this log's own stdout before the restart
   began).
